<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Life Adventures</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Small, Brown Bag.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write simple thank-you notes or greetings in cards using one-sentence picture cards. Interactive: Contribute a word to a class story or sentence, with help.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple, polite thank-you notes or greetings using familiar classroom vocabulary and adjectives (e.g., "Hello, friend! Thank you for the red pencil."). Can contribute words to a simple story describing classroom objects (e.g., "red," "green," and "pencil"). Can describe classroom objects in photos or drawings using adjectives and the present simple tense (e.g., "This is a big, blue book." or "The bag is small and brown.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Hello, everyone!" and displays various classroom objects (realia: a small brown bag, a red pencil, a big blue book, etc.). The teacher holds up an object and asks, "What is this?" encouraging choral responses. Then, the teacher says, "It's a small, brown bag." and asks students to repeat. The focus is on reviewing adjectives and nouns related to classroom items, preparing them for writing descriptions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture card of a red pencil. On the board, the teacher slowly writes: "Hello, friend! Thank you for the red pencil." The teacher points to each word as they write, saying it aloud. Then, the teacher points to a picture of a small brown bag and says, "This is a small, brown bag." The teacher writes the sentence clearly on the board, emphasizing "small," "brown," and "bag."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher points to the sentence "This is a small, brown bag." on the board.
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: "Is this a big bag or a small bag?" (Expected answer: "Small bag.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: "What color is the bag?" (Expected answer: "Brown.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher will use gestures and show the realia (small brown bag) to reinforce understanding.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a series of large picture cards depicting various classroom objects with simple, pre-written thank-you notes or descriptive sentences. For example:
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Card 1: Picture of a red pencil. Text: "Thank you for the red pencil."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Card 2: Picture of a big blue book. Text: "This is a big, blue book."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Card 3: Picture of a small brown bag. Text: "That's a small, brown bag."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Card 4: Picture of a yellow crayon. Text: "Hello, friend! This is a yellow crayon."</li>
                </ul>
                The genre is short, descriptive sentences and simple greeting/thank-you notes, with a polite register appropriate for "School Life Adventures."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Linguistic Competence - Employing polite expressions in greetings and descriptive sentences, using adjectives and the present simple tense. Pragmatic Competence - Describing objects with detail: "This is a small bag." "That's a big, blue book."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher shows each card and reads the sentence aloud.
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> After showing all cards, the teacher asks, "Are these about school things?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher holds up a card (e.g., "This is a big, blue book.") and then shows two real objects (a big blue book and a small red pencil). The teacher asks, "Which one matches the words?" Students point to the correct object.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing simple sentences describing classroom objects using adjectives (brown, red, blue, big, small) and the present simple tense ("This is," "That's a"). They will also practice writing simple thank-you notes and greetings using familiar vocabulary (pencil, book, bag).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice – Tracing and Copying:</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with dotted lines for tracing and copying. Each section features a picture of a classroom object (e.g., a red pencil, a big blue book, a small brown bag). Below each picture, there's a sentence to trace: "This is a red pencil." or "Thank you for the big book." Students trace the sentences first, then copy them onto provided lines below. The teacher circulates, providing individual help with letter formation and word spacing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled Practice – Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher distributes another worksheet with pictures of classroom objects and incomplete sentences. For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Picture of a green eraser. Sentence: "This is a ______ eraser." (Students write "green").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Picture of a small yellow crayon. Sentence: "That's a small, ______ crayon." (Students write "yellow").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Picture of a brown backpack. Sentence: "Thank you for the ______ backpack." (Students write "brown").</li>
                        </ul>
                        Students choose the correct adjective or noun from a small word bank provided at the top of the worksheet to complete each sentence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction – Shared Sentence Writing:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of two picture cards of different classroom objects (e.g., a big table and a small chair). On a shared piece of paper, each student takes turns writing one word to collectively form a sentence describing one of the objects or a simple greeting related to it (e.g., Student A writes "This", Student B writes "is", Student A writes "a", Student B writes "big", Student A writes "table", Student B writes "."). They then switch cards and repeat. The teacher observes, ensuring correct word usage and sentence structure, and facilitates peer-checking for legibility.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual creative writing of thank-you notes or greetings on small cards, inspired by "School Life Adventures."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. The teacher provides each student with several small blank cards (like mini greeting cards) and a collection of small picture cutouts of various classroom objects (e.g., a red pencil, a small brown bag, a blue book, a green eraser, a yellow crayon).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Students choose one picture cutout to paste onto a card.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Below the picture, students write a simple, polite thank-you note or a greeting using one sentence, incorporating adjectives and familiar classroom vocabulary. They can refer to the sentences practiced in Stage 3 or create their own.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Examples:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If they choose a red pencil: "Thank you for the red pencil."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If they choose a small brown bag: "Hello, friend! That's a small, brown bag."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If they choose a big blue book: "This is a big, blue book."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. The teacher encourages students to write legibly and focus on using the adjectives (small, brown, big, red, blue, green, yellow) and nouns (bag, pencil, book, eraser, crayon) correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">5. Students can optionally draw a small picture on the card too, reinforcing the visual-verbal connection.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">6. Students display their finished thank-you notes/greeting cards on a "Classroom Objects Gallery" board.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during production, complemented by a simple checklist rubric for the written thank-you notes/greeting cards.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' individual written thank-you notes/greeting cards produced in Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">✓ Uses one complete sentence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">✓ Includes target vocabulary (e.g., "bag," "pencil," "book").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">✓ Includes at least one adjective (e.g., "small," "brown," "red").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">✓ Attempts polite expression (e.g., "Thank you," "Hello").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">✓ Writing is legible (can be understood).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick "Exit Ticket" slip with a smiley face (😀), neutral face (😐), or sad face (☹️) indicating how they feel about writing the thank-you notes. They also respond to one of the following prompts by circling an option or drawing:
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write about a color." (Yes / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write 'Thank you'." (Yes / No)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "What new word did you write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "What was easy or difficult about writing?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well during the writing activities, especially regarding tracing and sentence completion? Were students able to independently form simple thank-you notes/greetings with adjectives? What adjustments are needed for students who struggled with letter formation or recalling vocabulary and adjectives for writing? (Perhaps more scaffolded sentence frames or a larger word bank for the next writing task).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This writing lesson prepares students for the final integration lesson (Lesson 5), where they will use their descriptive writing skills and knowledge of classroom objects to contribute to a class project or story, summarizing and presenting their learning about "School Life Adventures."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>